===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[blunder#Noun|blunder]], an [[error]].
#: {{synonyms|en|boo-boo|faux pas|fluff|gaffe|lapse|mistake|slip|stumble|Thesaurus:error}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Richard Lederer|chapter=Introduction|title=Fractured English: A Pleasury of Bloopers and Blunders, Fluffs and Flubs, and Gaffes and Goofs|edition=1st trade paperback|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Pocket Books|month=November|year=1996|page=xv|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xIBJ2zRyGnsC&pg=PR15|isbn=978-0-671-00036-3|passage=Why do my readers and informants so delight in '''bloopers''' and boo-boos, fluffs and flubs, and goofs and gaffes? {{...}} The humor in '''bloopers''' lies, in part, in the listener's awareness of the speaker's vulnerability. It is the very artlessness of linguistic lapses that makes them so endearing and makes us feel superior.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jeff Johnson|chapter=Responsiveness Bloopers|editor=Diane D. Cerra|title=GUI Bloopers: Don’ts and Do’s for Software Developers and Web Designers|location=San Francisco, Calif.|publisher=w:Morgan Kaufmann Publishers|year=2000|page=368|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=-kcKpqzbBR0C&pg=PA368|isbn=978-1-55860-582-4|passage=This chapter describes the most common responsiveness '''bloopers''' and explains why developers commit them. It is organized differently from the other '''bloopers''' chapters of this book because responsiveness '''bloopers''' are all closely related to one another; they are all really variations on the same underlying '''blooper''', with the same underlying reasons and solutions.}}
# {{lb|en|baseball|slang}} A [[fly ball]] that is [[weakly]] [[hit#Verb|hit]] just over the [[infielders]]. {{defdate|19th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|banjo hit|flare|Texas leaguer}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jimmy Powers|title=Baseball Personalities: The Most Colorful Figures of All Time|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=R. Field|year=1949|page=254|oclc=10396289|passage=Again the '''blooper''' wafted up to the plate. Ted waited and waited and then let loose. The ball rose in a high trajectory and sailed deep into the bullpen for a homer. The fans roared. The slugger had killed the '''blooper'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard F. Peterson|title=The Pirates Reader|location=Pittsburgh, Pa.|publisher=w:University of Pittsburgh Press|year=2003|page=139|isbn=978-0-8229-4199-6|passage=The '''blooper''', delivered with a shot-put motion, floated in an arc that reached twenty-five feet, then suddenly dropped across home plate in the strike zone.}}
# {{lb|en|film|informal}} A [[film#Verb|filmed]] or [[videotape#Verb|videotaped]] [[outtake]] that has [[record#Verb|recorded]] an [[amusing#Adjective|amusing]] [[accident]] and/or [[mistake#Noun|mistake]].
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=WLK|editors=David J. Bodenhamer and Robert G[raham] Barrows, with the assistance of David G[ordon] Vanderstel|title=The Encyclopedia of Indianapolis|location=Bloominton; Indianapolis, Ind.|publisher=w:Indiana University Press|date=31 December 1921|year_published=1994|page=1399|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=bg13QcMSsq8C&pg=PA1399|column=2|isbn=978-0-253-31222-8|passage=WLK. First radio station. On December 31, 1921, local engineer {{smallcaps|Francis F. Hamilton}}'s radio station, 9ZJ, signed on with an address from Mayor {{smallcaps|Samuel (Lew) Shank}}. Broadcasting from Hamilton's garage at 2011 North Alabama Street, Shank made the city's first radio '''blooper''': "Hamilton, do you mean to tell me that people can actually hear me over that damn' dingus?"}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Some Goof: Success through Mistakes|editor=Lee Zhito|magazine=[[w:Billboard (magazine)|Billboard: The International Music-record Newsweekly]]|location=Cincinnati, Oh.|publisher=The Billboard Publishing Company|date=24 August 1963|volume=75|issue=34|page=7|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=VgsEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA7|columns=1 and 3|oclc=644967835|passage=The 10th anniversary of the {{w|Kermit Schafer}} '''Blooper''' LP's on Jubilee will be marked by a brand-new '''blooper''' LP called "Washington '''Bloopers'''." Set is the 12th in the '''blooper''' series which started in 1953. {{...}} Schafer's '''blooper''' packages have become so popular that he has formed a '''Blooper''' Snooper Club. Members of the club get prizes for '''blooper''' contributions, and it will soon have a monthly newsletter.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Fabrice Robinet|title=Oh for the Days of the Making-Of Featurette — Seriously|work=The New York Times|date=2018-04-06|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/04/06/movies/dvd-extras.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=With DVDs steadily joining VHS cassettes as extinct technology, what has become of the fun, insightful mixed bag that movie fans came to know as bonus features — the audio commentaries, behind-the-scenes featurettes, '''bloopers''', deleted scenes and alternate endings?}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[gaff#Noun|gaff]]-[[rigged#Adjective|rigged]] [[fore-and-aft]] [[sail#Noun|sail]] set from and [[aft]] of the [[aftmost]] [[mast]] of a [[square-rigged]] [[ship#Noun|ship]]; a [[spanker]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=Yacht Racing|location=Chicago, Ill.|publisher=One-Design Yachtsman|year=1976|volume=15|page=30|column=3|issn=0276-2935|oclc=473159109|passage=Once the '''blooper''' is around the spinnaker, let out the '''blooper''' sheet completely (which collapses the '''blooper'''), race the '''blooper''' sheet forward and reset on the other side.}}
# {{lb|en|US|dated}} A [[radio#Noun|radio]] which [[interfere]]s with other radios, causing them to [[bloop#Verb|bloop]] ([[squeal#Verb|squeal]] [[loudly]]). {{defdate|from 1926}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Zeh Bouck|title=A Great Two-tube Receiver: Complete Instructions for Building a Compact and Efficient Radio Receiving Set which Gives Wonderfully Satisfactory Results|magazine=[[w:Boys' Life|Boys’ Life: The Magazine for All Boys]]|location=[Irving, Tx.]|publisher=w:Boy Scouts of America|month=March|year=1925|volume=XV|issue=3|page=51|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=W6Wmdnz1sTQC&pg=PA51|column=2|oclc=848277009|passage=Upon close tickler coupling, the detector tube will oscillate, in which condition the tuning of DX will be faciliated. At such times the amount of energy radiated from the antenna is negligible, and does not interfere with reception or neighboring devices. This Roberts set is, therefore, ''not'' a "'''blooper'''."}}
# {{lb|en|US|military|slang}} The Vietnam-era {{w|M79 grenade launcher}} (due to its distinctive report).

